WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.770
- Well, hello everyone,

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:13.020
and welcome to the second
installment of the webinar series

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.430
Doing Chinese History in a New Era.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:19.250
This series is organized by
Denise Ho at Yale University

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.190
and myself, Emily Baum, at UC Irvine.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:26.530
And it began last summer in
response to the persistent

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:29.610
and in some ways unprecedented challenges

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:32.280
that have been facing
researchers and scholars

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.580
in the Chinese studies field.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.210
So, this series has been
generously sponsored

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.040
by the Council on East Asian
Studies at Yale University

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.590
and Long U.S.-China
Institute at UC Irvine.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.870
We are also very grateful to
our long list of co-sponsors,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.550
and if you'll just bear
with me for a second,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.660
I'm gonna read through
them really quickly.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.360
Harvard University's Fairbank Center,

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.680
the Center for East Asian
Studies at Stanford University,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.630
The project on China's Global Sharp Power

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.320
at the Hoover Institution at Stanford,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.330
the Center for Asian
Research at York University,

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.570
the Department of History
at Simon Fraser university,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.250
the Institute of Asian Research

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.800
at the University of British Columbia,

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.160
the East Asian Studies program
at Johns Hopkins University,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.360
the East Asian Studies
program at UC Santa Cruz,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.490
the Global China Center

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.680
at Hong Kong University
of Science and Technology,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.110
and the Hong Kong Institute

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.520
for the Humanities and Social Sciences

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.453
at the University of Hong Kong.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.660
So in this session, we will
be focusing on the subject

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.000
of pivoting to a new research topic.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.320
Many of us, myself included,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.890
have had to dramatically
change or rethink the content

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.840
and scope of our research

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.730
in the face of challenges imposed

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.950
by COVID-19, archival closures,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.360
or simply increasing restrictions
on travel and free speech.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.100
To address some of these issues,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.610
we have invited four speakers
who are either completing

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.530
or who have recently completed
a thesis or dissertation,

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.980
and who have had to adapt their topics

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.620
because of changing research conditions.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.680
They are going to speak
about their experiences

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.840
and hopefully offer
some advice and guidance

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.070
for those of us who are
facing similar circumstances.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.890
So, each presenter will speak
for approximately 10 minutes,

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:22.940
and at the end of the session

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.610
we will reconvene for a moderated Q&amp;A.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.610
If you have a question at any point,

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:31.320
and you don't necessarily have
to wait for the Q&amp;A session,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.460
but please feel free to ask your question

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.670
using the Q&amp;A button at
the bottom of your screen.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.380
In total, this webinar should last

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.120
for approximately 75 minutes,
just to give you a heads up,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.540
and it will be recorded

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.280
in case you can't stay until the end.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.630
We will send out a link
to the YouTube video

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.800
in a follow-up email to all
registered participants.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.830
So, let us go ahead and get started.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.450
Speaking first will be Michael Collins.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.170
Michael is a researcher

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.010
focusing on China's global
governance initiatives.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.890
He was a research associate

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.980
at the Council on Foreign Relations,

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.010
and before that he received
his MA in Chinese history

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.650
from Peking University's Yenching Academy.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.410
So, I will turn it over
to you now, Michael.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:15.290
- Thanks so much.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.453
Let me share my screen.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.690
Great.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.410
So I guess the theme of my presentation

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.910
is Researching Chinese History:

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.590
Flexibility and Creativity.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.280
So to start,

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.680
my original idea was...

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.610
Or I began researching
on my master's thesis

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.150
in the fall of 2018.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.290
At the time, I was living on
Peking University's campus

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:45.530
as part of the Yenching Academy program.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.070
I originally intended for my thesis

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.530
to focus on Chinese anarchists

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.640
at the turn of the 20th century.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:53.750
I wanted to show that

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.780
rather than passively receiving anarchism

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.640
from Western sources, whether
it was in Paris or Japan,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.060
Chinese anarchists were
playing a very active role,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.400
and even leading

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.730
the East Asian anarchist movement

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.070
that was occurring at this period.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.710
To do this, I wanted to map
out Chinese anarchist networks

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.120
to demonstrate their
centrality to other East

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.260
and Southeast Asian anarchist movements,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.330
particularly in Korea and Vietnam.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.220
And I was really inspired to do this

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.590
by a few different sources,

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:32.460
but particularly by a book by
Arif Dirlik and Ming K. Chan

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.540
that focused on Shanghai's
Labor University,

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.840
which had an abundance of
anarchists, not just from Asia,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.590
but from all over the globe
who were working together

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.940
to try and think about how
to give both theoretical

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.770
and practical teaching to anarchists,

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:52.540
a growing cohort of anarchists in China.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:55.580
To do this research, I was
planning on using archives

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.850
at PKU and Fudan,

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:01.330
as well as some libraries
in the US and the UK.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:04.320
I also expected to be able to travel

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.820
particularly to Northeast
provinces of China

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.430
where there was a great number
of archives that were open

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.180
to look at cooperation between
North Korean anarchists

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:19.433
and American or and Chinese anarchists.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.940
So, pretty quickly on
in the research process

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:28.130
I encountered both bureaucratic hurdles,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.840
which I expected, but
also political hurdles

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.530
that prevented me from accessing archives,

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.830
archives which had previously
given me agreements

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.700
to access certain materials

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.783
came up with reasons why I
could no longer see them,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.993
and reasons that my
classmates were not facing.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:52.150
And heightened political sensitivities

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.860
around Chinese anarchists,

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:57.350
especially as kind of some
big historical anniversaries

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.290
were coming up in China

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.790
really tightened the access to archives,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:07.140
even at PKU, which was
surprising given my affiliation.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.300
And then, once COVID

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.440
became an issue in
China and then globally,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.033
and as US-China ties
really soured and fell out,

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:22.080
my ability to access any archives in China

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.220
were pretty closed as
I relocated to the US.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.890
And so, as I was

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.423
kind of reformulating my plan,

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.250
I took a moment to kind of
mourn the loss of the topic.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:41.260
And once I had kind of picked myself up

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:42.860
and gotten back in the race,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.520
I discussed with my thesis advisor

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.650
what would be feasible,

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.340
and he really encouraged me

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.610
to kind of not give up on the topic,

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.750
but just widen the aperture
of what I was studying

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.090
and to return to my
original literature review

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.950
to see if I could find
questions that would answer

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:01.920
the same question,

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.930
or give answers that I
was still interested in,

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:09.450
but would have materials that
I could get access to easily

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.560
and in a timely manner.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.700
I also assessed what was feasible
given COVID restrictions,

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.040
and at the time we really didn't know

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.350
when archives would reopen,

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.210
and so I just kind of assumed

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:24.430
that I would not be able to
go in-person to any archives

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:28.600
before my thesis was due for defense.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.320
And so, I really focused
on archival materials

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.230
in the US and the UK,
particularly resources

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:37.920
that had extensive digitized collections.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.310
And also I reached out to a
number of scholars in the field,

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.750
people whose books I was referencing,

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:47.390
scholars I had met at conferences

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:49.340
to not only ask their advice,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.940
but also to see if they
had primary sources

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.880
that I was looking for perhaps digitized

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.573
on their own computer
or in their own records.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:05.670
And so for the new topic,

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.280
as I was doing this review
a few weeks into it,

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.840
I noticed a relative
lack of scholarly work

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.730
on Chinese anarchists in America,

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:18.270
particularly by historians
of America's labor movements

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.060
and America's anarchist movements.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.830
And I, at the same time,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.980
I noticed a relative abundance
of primary source materials

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:31.980
which told a story that I was
intrigued to look into more,

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.160
and really gave me the feeling

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:35.730
that there was a lot more out there

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:39.620
that I could uncover through the research.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:43.510
So, I shifted gears a bit and
I focused my research topic

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:48.370
on anarchist groups started by
Chinese immigrants in America

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:50.670
and examined their participation

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.280
in and oftentimes their exclusion from

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:58.210
early 20th American labor
and anarchist movements.

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:00.640
And as a native of the Bay Area,

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:03.340
I was particularly
focused on San Francisco,

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.350
which had a very vibrant
Chinese immigrant community

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:10.300
and a very politically
active immigrant community.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:13.500
For this research, I am
deeply, deeply indebted

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:16.800
to the work of kind of
the master archivist

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.490
Him Mark Lai and the Berkeley
University Libraries,

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.510
which have fantastic collections,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.770
a lot of which are digitized,

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:28.323
which really enabled
me to do this research.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:34.850
And so, kind of over the
year and a half long process

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:35.700
of this research,

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.910
I've collected a number of resources

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.350
and thought about a few pieces of advice

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.340
that I hope are very useful.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.550
And I can kind of go in a bit
to what I found the strengths

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.370
of these sources were,

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:53.110
as well as kind of where
they might be lacking.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.320
And so, for the first four

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:58.680
that I have listed under the resources,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.380
all of these were fantastic
for accessing old publications,

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.790
newspapers, anarchist magazines,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.670
books that are no longer in circulation

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.930
or that are hard to come by.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.410
But especially with the
first listed source,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.580
I found that the search
terms you have to use,

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.380
or the process for finding materials

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.710
is not the most straightforward.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:24.270
Oftentimes, materials are not categorized

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.300
by their name or by their subject,

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.790
and so you really have
to search far afield

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:30.760
to find what you're looking for,

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:31.930
but once you find it,

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:33.540
they're usually free to access,

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:37.780
free to download, and
are incredibly helpful.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:41.100
And then the last two are a bit more niche

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.760
for people that are interested
in anarchist's movements,

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.920
whether they're in the
US or somewhere else.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.230
But I found that the
online Anarchist Library

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.060
and the Kate Sharpley Library

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.250
were fantastic for kind of giving me

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.820
both a general picture of the landscape,

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.650
but also for finding sources

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.400
that I didn't even know were out there.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:05.150
And they have dedicated
teams that curate the content

00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:10.150
and are happy to answer questions
or provide advice for you.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:13.010
And then in terms of advice,

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:15.550
the main thing that I
was told by my advisor

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:19.000
and I highly recommend was read broadly

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.130
because I felt pressure initially

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:22.740
to really decide on a topic,

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:26.270
and once I'd found it to
kind of dig down quickly

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.290
and kind of get to the work of writing.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.050
But when I stepped back,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.830
I realized that a lot of
the books that I'd read,

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:33.960
whether they were at undergrad

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.410
or through the course
of my master's program,

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:41.070
had tidbits of information or
sources that I found useful

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:45.080
once I went back to
re-write and re-research.

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:48.750
Secondly, think creatively
and re-evaluate.

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:52.900
And so for me, I went
from going from a more,

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:56.400
I would say, descriptive thesis topic

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:59.800
to one which was more
focused on historiography

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:03.070
and trying to kind of
fill in the narrative

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.150
of both the American labor
and anarchist movements,

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.363
and Chinese immigrant stories in the US.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:14.530
Next, I found it very
helpful to look broadly

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:16.960
through anthologies and appendices

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:21.330
because oftentimes there's kind
of hidden gems within there

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:23.790
that pointed me in the right direction.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:27.780
And then for the last,
the next two are related,

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.580
but contact scholars and librarians,

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:33.750
whether it's over Twitter,
LinkedIn, ScholarNet,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.350
and lean on your peers and wider network

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.480
because I found that by discussing
my topic with my cohort,

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.950
they were often able to
point me to a bookstore

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:46.490
in Manchester that had some of the sources

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:47.840
I was looking for.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:51.630
Or at one point we were going
through somebody's garage

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:53.720
who had these boxes from a library

00:12:55.148 --> 00:12:58.100
that they used to curate,
which has since closed.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:01.883
And then finally, I would say
to just adjust expectations,

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:06.520
I was, as I said, very
disappointed at the outset,

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:08.290
but once I kind of got through

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:12.380
the initial grief of the loss,

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:14.270
I was really happy with the process

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.740
and was able to produce something

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:20.330
that I was very proud to defend.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.710
So, thank you so much,

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:24.560
And I look forward to your questions.

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.510
- Great, thank you so much, Michael.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:31.800
Up next will be Bill Figueroa.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:34.170
Dr. Figueroa completed his PhD

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.020
at the University of Pennsylvania,

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.880
and he is currently a postdoctoral fellow

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:42.330
at the University of
Pennsylvania Middle East Center.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:43.923
So, Bill, go ahead.

00:13:45.204 --> 00:13:46.050
- Okay, great.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:48.213
Well, thank you for that introduction.

00:13:48.213 --> 00:13:49.700
I don't have a fancy PowerPoint,

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:54.530
but I'm just gonna try to just
share some of my experiences.

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.550
So, the title of the workshop is you know,

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:58.360
Pivoting to a New Research Topic,

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:00.370
and that's something I've
had to do a few times

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:03.260
through the process of finishing my PhD.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:04.950
And I'm really glad to
have the opportunity

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:06.440
to share my experiences with you all,

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:08.840
so thanks to Emily and to everyone else

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.433
who put the work in to put this together.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.900
My dissertation ended up
changing substantially

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.030
from how I originally conceived it

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:19.780
in part due to problems
in how I was framing it,

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:23.653
and in part due to lack of
access to archival sources.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:26.900
Even after I made a lot of these changes,

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:30.860
I had to finish the writing
process during the pandemic,

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:34.570
which also limited my ability
to track down new sources

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:37.300
or to revisit archives
as new questions came up,

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.240
but it also presented
some new opportunities

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.770
so I'm gonna try to kind of
take you through that process

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:44.990
going throughout this presentation.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:46.540
After going through all that,

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:48.970
I do think I have some
thoughts I can share

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.430
about how to be flexible with your topic,

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.480
and in your approach to
what sources you can use

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:56.190
and what kind of things
you can do with them.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.650
So, when I started graduate school,

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:01.530
I had it in my mind that
I really wanted to study

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:03.340
the history of Sino-Iranian relations,

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:06.630
you know, the relationship
between China and Iran.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.680
I had been studying Chinese history

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:13.260
and Chinese language as an
undergraduate for my masters.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:14.300
And during my PhD,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.050
I branched out to middle Eastern history,

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:17.740
and especially the history of Iran

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:19.700
and started studying Persian.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:23.540
You know, my goal was after
looking at the research

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.400
that had been done, it
was really clear to me

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:27.550
that most of it had been done by people

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:28.630
either coming, you know,

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:30.360
primarily coming from the China side

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.500
and a bit coming from the Iran side.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:34.900
And most people had only
really looked at sources

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.560
from one language or another,

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.570
and then otherwise been
relying on secondary sources

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:42.220
or works in English for everything else.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.880
So, you know, as I was
going through the literature

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:47.180
that had been done on the subject,

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:50.250
it wasn't long before
I came across anecdotes

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:52.420
about the popularity of Maoism

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:55.560
as a political orientation
amongst Iranian student radicals

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:57.050
in the 1960s.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.920
I was really intrigued by
the idea of Iranian Maoism,

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:02.280
which seemed to demand an explanation

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.560
and has been largely overlooked.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:08.370
And at least according to me,
underappreciated by historians

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:10.890
of both Chinese and Iranian history.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.040
So, my original dissertation
topic was on just that,

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:16.000
it was on Iranian Maoism, its history,

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.280
its particular, you know,

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:18.610
the particular activities

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:20.970
of a specific Maoist organizations,

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:22.360
Sazman-e Enghelabi,

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:25.120
which was a branch of the
main Iranian communist party,

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:26.380
the Tudeh Party,

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:29.190
and was run by dissident
students in Europe.

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:30.180
So Sazman-e Enghelabi,

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.650
which means the
revolutionary organization,

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:36.300
as in like the revolutionary
faction of the Tudeh Party

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.120
was a major force amongst
Iranian student politics

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.770
in both Europe and the
US in the 60s and 70s.

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:45.030
And I wanted to write
something like a thick history

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:47.010
of their organization,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:49.050
them as a social movement and their impact

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:53.180
on Iranian history as I charted out.

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:55.910
You know, and part of the
problem that I first encountered

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:59.690
was that this was a pretty
brief moment in history.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.560
You know, it was like mostly
their influenced peaked

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.680
from the late 60s to the early 70s,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:06.820
but I still really wanted
I was drawn to the story.

00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:08.650
Most intriguingly to me,

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:10.810
the group had ties to
the Chinese government

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.110
and then traveled to
China for revolutionary

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:14.690
and ideological training,

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:17.320
they seem to play a part in
China's international propaganda

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:19.560
efforts during the Cultural Revolution.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:21.360
And to me, you know,

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:23.540
the story of these like
would-be revolutionaries

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:24.930
was just honestly really cool,

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:27.340
It was the first thing
that I had discovered

00:17:27.340 --> 00:17:28.860
that I was like this is something

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:31.130
that people don't know
about that I can write about

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:33.860
that excites me in a
way, it was interesting,

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:37.160
it was new, I didn't quite
fully understand it at first.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:38.973
They had really cool, you know,

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:43.670
just sort of speeches and
pamphlets that they had put out,

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.460
just kind of like really
fascinating rhetoric

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:47.270
and I wanted to understand
where it was coming from,

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:50.170
how you had this kind of fusion

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:52.840
or cross fertilization of interests

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:57.690
of Islamists and radical
communists at the same time.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.470
So, you know, I was well
maybe a little bit dazzled

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:02.610
by the idea and it was and as a result

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.480
I ended up focusing pretty narrowly

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:05.600
on this topic alone at first,

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:08.050
and that was probably my first mistake.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:09.870
You know, it's good to keep an open mind

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:11.020
on the broader picture.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:14.720
So, you know, once I came
back down to earth about it,

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:16.620
I ran into two problems right away.

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.700
First was the problem of sources,

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:21.380
I couldn't access anything
official about this group.

00:18:21.380 --> 00:18:24.540
Chinese archives as Michael
mentioned during this period

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:28.120
2018 were reportedly, I didn't
get a chance to go and try,

00:18:28.120 --> 00:18:31.020
but from what I'd heard, they
were not open to researchers.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:33.080
And in any event I had
been told by another,

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:36.160
by a friend who had had
a personal interest,

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.870
you know, one of the people
I ended up interviewing

00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:41.310
that they had tried and
failed a couple of years ago

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:44.540
to access holdings related
to Sazman Enghelabi

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.780
at the Foreign Ministry
Archives in Beijing.

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:48.710
So I didn't think I was
gonna be any more successful

00:18:48.710 --> 00:18:49.650
than they were,

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:51.860
they kind of had more of
an even direct connection

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.400
than I did.

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:55.430
The subject is also a little sensitive

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:57.520
in light of modern Sino-Iranian relations,

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:01.020
and the official views of
the CCP on its malice past.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:04.640
I have actually been angrily
berated by party members

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:06.940
from a Chinese university
before at a conference

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:09.010
over just some objections they had

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:11.590
to the way I portrayed
Maoism as an ideology.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.700
So, you know, whenever you're
working on subjects like this,

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:15.740
it can be difficult to access archives.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:19.220
Researching in Iran is
similarly difficult,

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.530
you know, I think probably people,

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:23.210
anyone who follows the
news understands that,

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:24.520
you know, it's both not feasible

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.670
and not safe for Americans to do research

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:28.840
on sensitive subjects in Iran,

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:30.680
so I didn't really wanna
go down that route.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.770
The best bet for me was to interview

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:34.960
some of the participants
because they were still around,

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:37.320
some of them lived in Europe
and the United States,

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:40.610
but that required some networking
and interviews in Persian,

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:42.370
and my language skills were just,

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:44.160
they weren't quite up
to the task just yet.

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:45.880
You know, I could read,

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:48.830
but I wasn't quite ready to
conduct full-on interviews

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:51.520
and get the answers that I needed.

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:54.740
Secondly, my primary advisor,
Firoozeh Kashani-Sabet,

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:56.670
who I am really indebted for,

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:58.800
for both the direction and the quality

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:01.490
I think of the dissertation
I produced in the end.

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.290
She pointed out that Iranian Maoism

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:05.750
was at the end of the day a fascinating

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:07.100
but very narrow topic.

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:08.270
It was a niche interest

00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:10.200
within a subfield of Iranian studies,

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:12.520
mostly amongst historians
of the Iranian left.

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:13.700
It would probably grab,

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:16.030
you know, attract some
attention within China studies,

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:18.937
but Julia Lovell hadn't
written her book yet

00:20:18.937 --> 00:20:21.510
"Global Maoism", so there
wasn't as much attention

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:23.870
that idea as there is right now.

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:26.650
It also only covered, as I
said, a short period of time,

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:29.260
and while I was excited about
this idea of a deep dive,

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.490
she encouraged me to
think bigger and broader.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:34.230
I needed to adjust my topic
so that I'd be able to attract

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:36.510
the interest of a wide
variety of historians,

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:39.210
both within and without my subfield

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.930
in order to avoid pigeonholing myself

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:42.960
and to maximize my
chances on the job market.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.440
I mean, these are serious
things we have to think about.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.390
And, you know, being a historian
of both Iran and China,

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:52.130
I really wanted to avoid having
to lean too hard to one side

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:56.630
and to find myself in a position

00:20:56.630 --> 00:20:59.840
where I wasn't appealing to a broad enough

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:03.560
cross section of people to be successful

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.260
as someone who's not yet
either entirely Chinese

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:09.150
or entirely Middle East
studies when applying for jobs

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.880
in the China and Middle
East studies fields.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.450
So basically, I reworked my
project from the ground up

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:17.140
and instead of focusing
narrowly on Iranian Maoism,

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:20.350
I decided to carve out a bit
of a larger space for myself.

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:22.800
I noted that all, or nearly all studies

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.880
of Sino-Iranian history
started around 1972,

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.720
and focus on events after 1979.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:31.040
I strongly suspected for
my background reading

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:33.600
and from the few scraps of
sources I had come across

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:34.870
that there were substantial connections

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:36.960
in earlier periods as well.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:39.500
So, I set out to write a survey
of Sino-Iranian relations

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:42.220
from the constitutional period
to these Islamic Revolution

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:44.850
from 1905 to 1979,

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:47.620
tracing different moments of
both official and unofficial

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:51.210
Sino-Iranian interactions
from the constitutional period

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:54.020
to interactions between nationalist China

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:58.190
under Chiang Kai-shek
and the early nationalist

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:02.000
government of Reza Khan/Reza Shah in Iran,

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:06.880
and then moving into his
sort of Cold War politics

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:09.140
of the 1950s and 1960s.

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:10.650
You know, this is a lot broader

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:14.290
and I was able to keep Iranian
Maoism as a chapter later on,

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:16.660
and it freed me up to find
new topics to focus on

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:17.810
for the earlier period.

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:19.960
You know, at first glance you would think

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:21.620
that this would actually
make it harder to study

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:22.990
given the lack of sources,

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:25.820
but it actually ended up giving
me a lot more to work with.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:27.690
Once I realized that there
were resources I could use

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:29.020
outside of archives,

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:31.370
especially for those of you
working on the late 19th,

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:32.890
early 20th centuries,

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:36.700
newspapers, travelogs, memoirs
are an incredible source

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:39.940
for day to day events that
have gone undocumented

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:42.400
by historians, especially for those of you

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:45.020
who are looking to do something similar,

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:46.660
you know, to Sino-Iranian relations

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:50.620
perhaps to look at another
less commonly explored aspect

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:51.700
of China's foreign policy

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:53.730
or relations with the rest of the world.

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:55.740
I mean, everyone's doing global
history right now, right?

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:58.120
That's what we're all supposed to do.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:00.750
You know, telegraphs allowed
people from all over the world

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:03.520
during this time to discuss and
learn about foreign affairs.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:06.730
And I found newspaper records
of Iranian constitutionalists

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:08.040
discussing China,

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:10.570
Chinese constitutionalist
inspired by Persia,

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:12.550
Iranian diplomats in Shanghai,

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:14.980
Chinese merchants petitioning
the Republican government

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:16.590
for a trade agreement with Iran,

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:18.730
Iranian editorials on Chiang Kai-shek.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:20.650
I mean, it was really just a lot

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:22.430
of different things I found.

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:24.780
So, I think my first tip is
really just keep an open mind

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:26.300
about where you can find your sources

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:30.080
and what constitutes a
useful source to you.

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:32.260
It's especially relevant
for Chinese history

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:33.410
because Chinese newspapers

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:36.240
are readily available in digital form.

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:37.523
There is a number of
archives that you can access

00:23:37.523 --> 00:23:39.370
through a university subscription

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:41.730
if you're at a school
that supports research

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:42.610
into Chinese history,

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:44.630
there's a good chance
your library has them,

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:46.490
that's how I've always accessed them.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:49.040
Through this, you can find
Chinese and English language

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:51.230
newspapers from and published in China

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:53.570
from the late 19th century
to the 20th century.

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:55.860
For me, that's been an
absolute treasure trove

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.010
of sources about events that,

00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:59.190
you know, otherwise
I'd have no idea about.

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:01.650
And they can be searched
very easily in digital form,

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:03.190
which makes things a lot easier.

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:04.930
It's also true about Persian newspapers,

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.679
although they're less readily digitized.

00:24:07.679 --> 00:24:09.860
Travelogs are also a
great thing to hunt down,

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:12.670
you have surprising amounts of information

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:15.530
buried in travelogs about
day to day activities

00:24:15.530 --> 00:24:17.233
of people in different places.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:20.300
Some things I did find in
archives like pamphlets,

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:21.810
propaganda posters,

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:23.730
there's a lot of material
related to this stuff

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:26.190
if you are interested at
the International Institute

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:28.420
of Social History in Amsterdam,

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:29.930
the Hoover Institute in California,

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:31.720
where I got to do some research.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:33.130
Oh, you do need to be able to read Persian

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:34.170
for the most part,

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:37.360
they do have a couple of
things in English as well.

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:38.860
When I was there, I did make,

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:41.870
I made it a habit of going for
kind of brief archival trips

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:44.500
and digitizing as much
as I could on my own

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:47.400
while I was there and taking
it home to read later.

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.140
And it's a really good thing I did that

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:51.430
because shortly after I
got back from the archives

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:53.563
and set to writing, the pandemic hit.

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:56.900
So, you know, at first that
made things a lot more difficult

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:59.000
because or at least in theory

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:00.600
because I couldn't go
to any other archives,

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.520
as I said, as questions came up.

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.360
But in some ways it did kind of end up

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:05.600
being a blessing in disguise

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:08.090
other than the obvious fact
that there was literally nothing

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:10.771
else to do except work on my dissertation,

00:25:10.771 --> 00:25:12.480
(laughs) which may have
been a larger factor

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:15.020
in me actually completing
it than I'd like to admit.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:17.370
There was also the fact
that even more sources

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:19.270
were being digitized and made accessible

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:20.470
due to the pandemic.

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:23.120
I also turned to networking, social media,

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.520
I learned about this opportunity
from Twitter (laughs).

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.690
So, you know, that's actually
been a surprisingly good

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:32.830
source of not just
information and networking,

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:35.560
but also just people who
have a personal interest

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:36.520
in the things that you're studying,

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:39.320
who might be able to supply
you with surprising sources.

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:42.150
Also just reaching out to scholars

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:43.610
I met at virtual conferences

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:45.830
or who seemed like they will
be interested in my work.

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:48.290
Virtual conferences made
it really easy to meet

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:51.490
and develop relationships with colleagues

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:53.430
in Iran and China in particular.

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:55.220
And some of my most interesting finds

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:57.000
have come from a Chinese student,

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:00.390
who's studying at University
of Tehran that I met,

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:01.940
who has access to a lot of materials

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:05.120
that I just wouldn't otherwise
be able to get myself.

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:07.290
So most of these things like digitization

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:08.300
and virtual conferences,

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:10.715
they're not going anywhere even
after the pandemic is over,

00:26:10.715 --> 00:26:12.520
they're definitely gonna stick around

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:15.060
so you can still take advantage of that.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:17.300
My time is definitely
up, so just to summarize,

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:18.630
try to think broadly,

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:21.290
be flexible in what
kinds of sources you use,

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:23.130
don't be afraid to open up your topic

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:25.410
and try to break completely new ground

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:27.780
because if your research succeeds in that,

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:29.480
it'll be that much more valuable.

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:33.090
Make good use of digital
sources, work with what you have,

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:35.290
virtual conferences are
a great way to network.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.270
And of course, reach out
to scholars in your fields

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:40.130
who might be interested in
your work, read their work.

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:42.900
A lot of the ideas that I came up with

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:46.350
for how to frame my ideas came
from reading other scholars,

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:48.480
Reem Abou-El-Fadl especially,

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:51.980
I'm very indebted to her
work on foreign relations,

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:55.500
and talk to them and talk to your advisor

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.140
about how to frame your topic.

00:26:57.140 --> 00:26:59.130
You know, senior scholars
will often see things

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:00.380
and issues that you might miss

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:02.380
because you're not as deeply embedded

00:27:02.380 --> 00:27:03.510
in the field as they are.

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:04.520
So, thank you very much,

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:06.543
and I hope you guys enjoyed my rambling.

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:11.080
- Great, thank you so much, Bill.

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:14.230
I've already started to see a
couple of overlapping themes

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:16.820
between your presentation
and Michael's presentation,

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:19.280
the need to read broadly, to network,

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:20.500
to reach out to other scholars,

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:22.540
and to also just be on
the constant lookout

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:24.010
for new digitized sources,

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:27.620
which seems to be proliferating every day.

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:30.490
Okay, so speaking next will be Yi Ci Lo.

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:33.250
Yi Ci is currently finishing
up her dissertation

00:27:33.250 --> 00:27:34.840
at UC Irvine,

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:37.690
and in the fall she'll
become a post-doctoral fellow

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:39.250
at the Schwarzman Center

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:42.050
at Tsinghua University in Beijing.

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:44.085
So, go ahead, Yi Ci.

00:27:44.085 --> 00:27:46.410
- Great, thank you.

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:49.380
So, good evening,
afternoon, morning everyone.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:52.100
My name is Yi Ci and I am
finishing my dissertation

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:54.740
at the University of California Irvine.

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.950
So, thanks Professors
Emily Baum and Denise Ho

00:27:57.950 --> 00:27:59.380
for organizing this webinar.

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:01.840
I'm right at the tail end of grad school,

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:03.830
so it feels like a good moment

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:05.760
to be reflecting on the twists and turns

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:07.350
of my research journey.

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:09.910
And hopefully, it's
something that'll be helpful

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:12.010
for grad students who are
just beginning to grapple

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:13.900
with dissertation research

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:15.900
in these challenging circumstances.

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:18.493
So, I'm going to try
to share my screen now.

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:21.370
My original project was a study

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:24.050
about the early history
of blood transfusion

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:26.880
and blood banking in 20th century China.

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:30.710
One of my tentative titles
was Unnatural Circulations:

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:33.290
Blood Transfusion and
Banking in Modern China.

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:35.130
I proposed to track changing medical

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:36.710
and cultural definitions of blood

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:39.023
as the practices of blood transfusion,

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:42.410
and then blood banking were introduced

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:45.900
and then gradually normalized
across the 20th century.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:47.960
This process started
early in the 20th century

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:50.170
when biomedical doctors
came up with tables,

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:51.443
that you can see here,

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:56.290
explaining basic facts about
blood types in transfusion.

00:28:56.290 --> 00:28:57.700
Like people in other places,

00:28:57.700 --> 00:28:59.880
people in China were initially suspicious

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.760
and they were fearful about
moving blood between bodies.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:04.080
But some of the consternation

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:07.160
also came from very culturally
specific understandings

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:07.993
of blood as (indistinct),

00:29:07.993 --> 00:29:11.520
and as something that was
connected to bloodline descent.

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:15.000
I had hoped to untangle
the uneasy coexistence

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:17.190
of these multiple definitions of blood

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:21.290
as medical professionals
and political leaders

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:22.840
called on people to give blood.

00:29:23.772 --> 00:29:27.250
Naturalizing blood donation
was a very long process

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:28.970
that stretched beyond the triumph

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:32.140
of the Chinese Communist Party in 1949.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:33.620
In the People's Republic of China,

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:35.930
one of the key events I
was hoping to write about

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:39.750
was a large scale government-run
blood donation campaign

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:43.130
in the 1960s on the eve of
the Cultural Revolution,

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:45.440
just when there was a
rising tide of consciousness

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:46.790
about bloodline theory,

00:29:46.790 --> 00:29:49.300
the idea that socialist
revolutionary credentials

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.630
could be passed down
through the bloodline.

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:54.570
So, that's what I proposed to do

00:29:55.720 --> 00:29:58.970
when I hit never tried to
get into a Chinese archive

00:29:58.970 --> 00:30:01.250
or tried to get people in Chinese archives

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:03.220
to give me access to documents

00:30:03.220 --> 00:30:04.850
about the Cultural Revolution,

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:08.010
which I found out was not a simple request

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:11.223
when I tried to do these
things in 2018 and 2019.

00:30:12.610 --> 00:30:13.790
The big problem really

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:18.010
was the post-1949 solstice.

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:21.220
When I went to conduct
pre-dissertation research in China,

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:23.850
it was obvious that
getting access to documents

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:26.050
about things that happen after 1949

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:29.180
was going to be a very big
problem for my project.

00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:31.060
When I talked to people about my work,

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:34.970
I got the impression that
accessing the PRC documents,

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:38.520
especially documents that touched
on the Cultural Revolution

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:42.260
was going to be very
difficult to impossible.

00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:45.100
And looking back, they were really right

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:46.890
to be quite discouraging

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:50.820
because when I did make it
into multiple Chinese archives,

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:52.733
I just couldn't get access to the sources

00:30:52.733 --> 00:30:55.670
that I wanted to read
about blood transfusion

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:57.883
and blood donation in Mao's China.

00:30:59.879 --> 00:31:00.820
At that point, of course,

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:03.900
I consider sort of stopping
my narrative at 1949

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:04.890
with the dissertation,

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:07.310
but ultimately I decided that

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:11.930
the development in the
PRC was so significant

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:14.960
and so integral to telling a full story

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:17.540
of how blood transfusion came to China,

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:21.020
that the project would be
fundamentally incomplete

00:31:21.020 --> 00:31:23.220
if I stopped at 1949.

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:25.360
So given the constraints,
I returned to the archives

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:26.300
looking for any topic,

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:29.070
and eventually I stumbled
onto units of measurement

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:30.563
and metrological reform.

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:33.490
Measuring Up to Modernity

00:31:33.490 --> 00:31:34.760
the title of my dissertation

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:38.010
tracks how the existence
of multiple systems

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:40.390
and practices of measurement in China

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:42.380
came to be perceived as a problem

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:43.640
by the late 19th century,

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:46.820
and how the Nanjing government
worked furiously to design

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:49.260
and then enforce a single
system of measurement

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:53.270
throughout China into
the 1930s and the 1940s.

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:56.330
So with this topic, the
arc kind of sits squarely

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:58.310
in the first half of the 20th century

00:31:58.310 --> 00:32:01.490
so I dodged the post-1949 problem.

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:04.110
At the archives, I found
that when I asked people

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:07.600
for records about metrological
reform and administration,

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:09.240
they tended to be quite bemused.

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:13.260
And I have had more than
one person been so curious

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:15.110
that they had to ask me why I was reading

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:16.330
what I was reading,

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:17.163
but the important thing

00:32:17.163 --> 00:32:20.040
is they always gave me the
files that I asked for.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:22.940
And this was a drastically
different reaction

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:25.130
compared to when I went into archives

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:28.180
and asked for documents
related to blood transfusion

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:30.463
and banking in Mao's China.

00:32:31.330 --> 00:32:33.790
In terms of sources like Bill,

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:36.240
I was I'm very fortunate
that there's a wealth

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:39.510
of published sources that
track official scientific,

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:41.270
popular and foreign thinking

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:43.570
about the state of measurement in China.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:45.698
Once the Nanjing government
announced the reform

00:32:45.698 --> 00:32:47.960
in 1928 and 29, it also published

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:50.840
a lot of public education
materials and instructions

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:53.920
about how to use the new
system of measurement.

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:57.090
Objections to and
disputes about the system

00:32:57.090 --> 00:32:59.070
were also recorded and published

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:00.530
rather than archival sources,

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:02.680
so these are a lot of the
periodicals and newspapers

00:33:02.680 --> 00:33:04.320
that were publish that at time.

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:06.860
And these sources allowed me
to examine the ways in which

00:33:06.860 --> 00:33:09.550
different groups responded to
the government's insistence

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:13.040
that they all use a single
system of measurement.

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:14.160
And in fact, I have to say

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:16.600
that I had a lot of fun
working with these sources

00:33:16.600 --> 00:33:18.710
because there was a variety of people

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:21.150
who were in fact, very
invested in measurement,

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:24.020
and they wrote very
passionately and heatedly

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:25.933
about metrological reform.

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:28.730
Measurement was also the kind of topic

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:31.460
I read in the margins of
many kinds of sources.

00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:33.590
For instance, it was easy
for me to check the units

00:33:33.590 --> 00:33:37.100
that people used when they
report economic information,

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:41.670
or when newspapers reported on
track and field competitions.

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:44.330
So, in all I could access
a very healthy pool

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:46.320
of published sources, which was a relief

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:49.510
because of how tight
archival access had become

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:52.210
since I started grad school
and dissertation research.

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:54.810
But in terms of archives,

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:56.160
I was able to work with the collections

00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:58.340
at the second historical
archives in Nanjing.

00:33:58.340 --> 00:34:01.330
And this was really
important archive for me

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:02.163
because of the topic

00:34:02.163 --> 00:34:03.500
and because I was writing about

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:06.030
the Kuomintang's Nanjing-based regime.

00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:08.360
I faced the usual challenges
that you would face

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:09.560
in a Chinese archive,

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:11.610
you know, I couldn't read certain files

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:13.460
that I wanted to read,

00:34:13.460 --> 00:34:16.310
they wouldn't print certain
pages that I wanted printed.

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:19.450
But overall, I managed to
get a lot of key sources

00:34:19.450 --> 00:34:22.650
about early internal
discussions when officials

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.630
were still designing the
system of measurement

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:27.090
in the 1910s and the 1920s.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.110
And I also got some really great sources

00:34:29.110 --> 00:34:30.560
about the really early days

00:34:30.560 --> 00:34:32.860
of pushing this system
of measurement through

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:35.230
when they were trying to
figure out how they were going

00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:37.370
to create the original standards

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:39.500
and to certify the authenticity

00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:42.010
and the accuracy of the new standards

00:34:42.010 --> 00:34:43.660
for China's weights and measures.

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:47.310
When my archival
researcher was interrupted

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:48.920
by the global pandemic,

00:34:48.920 --> 00:34:51.300
I also went to two archives in Taipei,

00:34:51.300 --> 00:34:54.770
the National Archives and
the Kuomintang Archives.

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:55.623
At the National Archives.

00:34:55.623 --> 00:34:57.980
I was mainly able to get
sources about methodological

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:00.330
standardization when the
Kuomintang took the campaign

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:02.550
to Taiwan after 1945.

00:35:02.550 --> 00:35:06.480
So, in that strange 1945-49 interregnum,

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:08.230
the Kuomintang told people in Taiwan

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:10.820
that they should abandon
Japanese units of measurement

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:12.730
and embrace Chinese weights and measures

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:14.740
because they were really Chinese

00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:17.163
and they were now reunited
with the motherland.

00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:20.700
The National Archives also
had a wealth of documents

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:23.920
about the difficulties of
enforcing the new system

00:35:23.920 --> 00:35:25.150
of measurement in different parts

00:35:25.150 --> 00:35:26.520
and different sectors in Taiwan.

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:28.170
So, it really gave me a new case study

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:30.610
of the difficulties and realities

00:35:30.610 --> 00:35:33.780
of enforcing a new system of
measurement on the ground.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:34.900
At the Kuomintang archives,

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:36.840
I found some records about
the early discussions

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:38.430
of metrological reform,

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:40.590
but also lots of interesting documents

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.410
about other sorts of standardizations,

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:44.570
which the Kuomintang tried to carry out

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:47.450
like getting people to
celebrate the New Year

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:48.803
on the 1st of January.

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:52.010
Looking back at the process,

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:54.150
I would say two things to
people who find themselves

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:55.830
in the position of doing research

00:35:55.830 --> 00:35:58.840
in this particular source-scarce era.

00:35:58.840 --> 00:36:00.410
I think it's important to keep looking,

00:36:00.410 --> 00:36:02.290
especially where it's possible.

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:04.750
Of course, you have to keep
trying to get archival sources,

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:05.890
but in the meantime,

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:07.180
you really also have to look

00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:09.480
at what's publicly accessible to you.

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:11.070
Personally, I've always found it fruitful

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:12.920
to check out like special collections

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:15.490
or the reading rooms
dedicated to modern history

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:17.560
at libraries in China.

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:19.710
I always find good public
education materials

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:23.293
about public health or popular
science from the 1930s,

00:36:23.293 --> 00:36:27.550
50s, or 80s, which are all
good jumping off points for me.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:29.850
I would also say that
at this particular time,

00:36:31.051 --> 00:36:34.830
it makes a lot of sense to
look at the possibilities

00:36:34.830 --> 00:36:36.960
in Taiwan, especially
the National Archives

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:39.240
since you can such it's database

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.663
to get a sense of whether
it would make sense

00:36:41.663 --> 00:36:43.360
for you to go down to Taipei in-person

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:45.270
to work at this archive.

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:46.950
Second, I would say that,

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:49.720
you know, you really have to
keep reading with an open mind,

00:36:49.720 --> 00:36:51.110
anything you get to read.

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:53.970
If you can't get the sources
you originally set out to find,

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:55.810
find something else and read that,

00:36:55.810 --> 00:36:58.380
and be prepared to follow any source

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:01.370
that you find interesting
where it may lead you.

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:03.780
The hard truth is that you have to work

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:05.040
with the sources you can get,

00:37:05.040 --> 00:37:08.030
you can't produce a dissertation
with sources can't get.

00:37:08.030 --> 00:37:09.870
So, all the sauces you can get,

00:37:09.870 --> 00:37:11.680
even if they're not about
the original topics,

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:14.440
you should read them because
you might not write this

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:15.460
as the dissertation,

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:18.410
you think that you envision writing,

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:20.633
but it might be a really great one anyway.

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:23.290
So, that's all I have for you today,

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:24.890
and hopefully that's what happened to me

00:37:24.890 --> 00:37:26.250
in the last three years,

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:28.550
and I hope that it's a source of hope

00:37:28.550 --> 00:37:31.240
and comfort to people rather than dismay.

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:33.750
And I look forward to
answering your questions later.

00:37:33.750 --> 00:37:34.583
Thank you.

00:37:36.290 --> 00:37:38.110
- Thank you so much, Yi Ci.

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:40.750
And I have to say just selfishly,

00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:42.640
I hope that unnatural circulations

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:46.400
will become your second book,
even if it is not your first.

00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:49.840
Okay, so last but not
least is Tullia Fraser.

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:51.960
Tullia recently completed
a master's degree

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:54.850
in museum and artifacts
studies at Durham University,

00:37:54.850 --> 00:37:57.750
where she also did her BA in archeology.

00:37:57.750 --> 00:37:59.520
She's currently a project associate

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:01.840
with the University Museum and Art Gallery

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:03.870
at the University of Hong Kong.

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:05.920
So, go ahead Tullia.

00:38:05.920 --> 00:38:07.720
- Thank you for the kind introduction.

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:11.010
Today, I am delighted to be
talking about my experience

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:14.340
with my completing my MA
Museum and Artifacts Studies

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:15.810
at Durham University in the UK.

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:17.610
And I really wanted to do a topic

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:20.770
that was really personal
to my own background,

00:38:20.770 --> 00:38:23.010
looking at basically how
the Chinese diasporas

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:26.023
were displayed in British museum spaces.

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:31.013
But at first, I actually
wanted to focus on Newcastle,

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:33.590
partly because I was based there,

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:36.810
I was connected there,
and I wanted to evaluate

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:40.080
a specific project called
Destination Tyneside,

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:43.440
which was the only museum
display that was permanent

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:45.490
in the UK on the topic of migration.

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.390
And it was informed by a really robust

00:38:47.390 --> 00:38:49.230
public participation scheme.

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:51.810
So, it was really unique and
it was situated in a city

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:55.343
that had a long presence
of Chinese people.

00:38:57.220 --> 00:38:58.690
So to that end,

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:01.810
I wanted to firstly review
historically the migration

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:03.980
from Hong Kong to Newcastle,

00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:08.980
engaging with primary
resources and archives.

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:11.400
And I wanted to sit in the gallery

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:14.370
over a course of several
months with my field visits,

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:16.310
looking at maybe visitor observation

00:39:16.310 --> 00:39:17.290
or even talking to them

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:20.050
to see what they're engaging
with, and what they're not.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:21.790
And finally, I was really keen to talk

00:39:21.790 --> 00:39:24.950
to actual members of the
Chinese community there,

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:25.783
talk about their life,

00:39:25.783 --> 00:39:28.670
but also talk about if the
museum actually plays a role

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:31.913
in their sense of belonging
or placemaking in the area.

00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:34.390
So, I pretty much hit the first hurdle

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:38.180
when the first national
lockdown was imposed in the UK,

00:39:38.180 --> 00:39:40.950
archives and museums were closed,

00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:42.820
there was a stay at home order,

00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:44.310
and the Hong Kong government,

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:46.890
which is Hong Kong is where I'm from,

00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:49.830
they issued a recommendation
for students to return.

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:51.740
And in the end, because
of personal reasons,

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:54.143
I actually did have to
physically relocate.

00:39:57.060 --> 00:40:00.513
So, I was quite stubborn,

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:04.270
I was quite keen to save

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:06.960
as much of the original topic as I could.

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:09.550
And when I realized that
archives in Hong Kong and the UK

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:12.690
were closed, and that
the digitized resources

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:13.823
weren't exactly what I needed,

00:40:13.823 --> 00:40:16.540
I pretty much had to
abandon that objective.

00:40:16.540 --> 00:40:20.070
I still wanted to evaluate
Destination Tyneside,

00:40:20.070 --> 00:40:21.990
and this was when I looked up ETHoS,

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:26.530
which is a database compiling
the doctoral projects

00:40:26.530 --> 00:40:29.520
by different researchers in the UK.

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:32.160
So, on ETHoS I found a project

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:35.470
that had recently been
completed only in 2019

00:40:35.470 --> 00:40:39.960
and had amazing field notes
about Destination Tyneside.

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:42.840
So, I actually managed to find
the researcher on LinkedIn

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:45.220
and we connected, and she
just so kindly provided

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:46.280
not just her field notes,

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:49.233
but images, and even
transcripts of the videos

00:40:49.233 --> 00:40:51.083
that were being used in the display.

00:40:51.940 --> 00:40:55.380
Through email, I also
spoke with the curator

00:40:55.380 --> 00:40:57.100
of Destination Tyneside

00:40:57.100 --> 00:40:59.730
and she not only had press images for me,

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:02.050
she even physically went
down to the gallery for me

00:41:02.050 --> 00:41:04.990
to take photos, which was just brilliant.

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:06.580
I also had family and colleagues

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:08.733
who kindly offered to visit on my behalf,

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:10.930
but the main problem for that

00:41:10.930 --> 00:41:13.980
was that there was no projected
reopening dates at all

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:16.763
for museums or archives in the UK.

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.580
I was still keen to
speak to Chinese people

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:23.710
and how they felt about the gallery.

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:25.983
I tried to do this over Zoom,

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:29.810
but one thing that became apparent

00:41:29.810 --> 00:41:32.250
was the age of my target group.

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.450
I was looking at a wave of migration

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:37.590
that was triggered by the
UK colonial government

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:39.170
in Hong Kong in 60s,

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:42.110
encouraging rural populations
here to move over to the UK.

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:43.720
So, these people at their youngest

00:41:43.720 --> 00:41:45.020
would have been at their 50s,

00:41:45.020 --> 00:41:47.943
provided that they went over
there when they were children.

00:41:48.890 --> 00:41:51.050
And the transition to online communication

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:52.690
has not been gentle for any of us,

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:55.230
but especially this target group

00:41:55.230 --> 00:41:58.160
where people were I think the
oldest person I tried to reach

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:00.160
was nearing her 90s.

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:03.070
So, my physical location

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:06.640
meant that I couldn't physically
go up to talk to people,

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:09.180
and it became quite clear
that my initial strategy

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:11.570
to sort of mitigate my circumstances

00:42:11.570 --> 00:42:14.750
didn't generate enough
data to support my project,

00:42:14.750 --> 00:42:16.810
and I had to accept pretty much

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:19.030
that the topic wasn't going to work.

00:42:19.030 --> 00:42:21.100
And in hindsight, I would disagree,

00:42:21.100 --> 00:42:23.440
but in the moment there
was very much a feeling

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:26.950
that I had failed and it wasn't nice,

00:42:26.950 --> 00:42:28.093
it wasn't nice at all.

00:42:29.910 --> 00:42:32.150
So, I started looking at changing topics

00:42:32.150 --> 00:42:34.450
and I think it's a recurring theme

00:42:34.450 --> 00:42:37.380
amongst the fellow panelists
that we wanted to remain

00:42:37.380 --> 00:42:39.210
in a similar ballpark for a topic

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:42.300
due to personal connection,

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:46.420
but also because we had
done the research already.

00:42:46.420 --> 00:42:50.353
So, I basically took a
very erotic damage control

00:42:50.353 --> 00:42:54.770
sort of approach where
identified the biggest problems

00:42:54.770 --> 00:42:57.530
on my original topic and tried to fix it.

00:42:57.530 --> 00:43:01.150
So, I widened my scope to
evaluate any displays of migration

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:02.700
in any part of the UK.

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:04.550
So, I stuck with Destination Tyneside,

00:43:04.550 --> 00:43:07.510
but I also came across this
project called Arrivals,

00:43:07.510 --> 00:43:11.920
which was a sort of public-led project

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:13.283
by a photographer,

00:43:14.750 --> 00:43:16.360
just featuring different people

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:19.450
who had recently moved to
different parts of the UK.

00:43:19.450 --> 00:43:20.640
So, not only did I have

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:22.750
the researcher's detailed field notes,

00:43:22.750 --> 00:43:27.100
I also had the curators
photos from both projects.

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:31.290
I also started basically just
going through ancient websites

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:34.070
for hours and hours and
I found press previews,

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:36.370
which gave me a different
perspective on the gallery.

00:43:36.370 --> 00:43:39.060
And then I went on even
TripAdvisor, Twitter,

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:41.920
and actually started
finding the public reviews

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:44.380
on these displays,

00:43:44.380 --> 00:43:45.290
which was brilliant

00:43:45.290 --> 00:43:47.620
because I had now
literally different angles

00:43:47.620 --> 00:43:50.200
and perspectives of the galleries.

00:43:50.200 --> 00:43:53.310
So, trends started to show
up and it became clear

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:56.460
that there was a chance
to talk about the methods

00:43:56.460 --> 00:43:57.990
in displaying migration.

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:00.250
So I focused, especially on temporary

00:44:00.250 --> 00:44:02.540
versus permanent displays

00:44:02.540 --> 00:44:05.083
through objects or photographs as mediums.

00:44:06.040 --> 00:44:07.740
I also wanted to talk to anyone

00:44:07.740 --> 00:44:10.670
who was involved in displaying
migration in the UK.

00:44:10.670 --> 00:44:13.780
And I was really fortunate to
be able to contact the curator

00:44:13.780 --> 00:44:14.990
of Destination Tyneside

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:17.290
and the creator of the Arrivals project,

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:19.820
and we actually had brilliant interviews,

00:44:19.820 --> 00:44:21.870
but it also meant that

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:24.490
apart from the varying
perspectives on the gallery,

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:27.100
I now had firsthand information

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:30.660
on what the expectations

00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:32.863
the ideal project would have been.

00:44:36.110 --> 00:44:37.470
Like I said, quite stubborn,

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:39.880
I still wanted to interview
any Chinese visitors

00:44:39.880 --> 00:44:41.530
who saw these displays,

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:43.620
and it was really challenging
because of lockdown

00:44:43.620 --> 00:44:46.610
on both sides, and the time
difference between the regions,

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:48.950
which was sometimes seven
hours or eight hours.

00:44:48.950 --> 00:44:51.260
And I mentioned briefly the
age of the target group,

00:44:51.260 --> 00:44:54.560
but soon enough occupation came into play,

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:57.160
the UK government rolled out a scheme

00:44:57.160 --> 00:44:58.840
called Eat Out to Help Out,

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:02.040
which is this scheme to sort
of stimulate the economy

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:04.430
by reopening eateries.

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:07.840
And the people that I was looking
at and wanted to work with

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:11.370
were still heavily
involved in catering, FNB,

00:45:11.370 --> 00:45:12.720
the important export of food,

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:14.630
or even just running supermarkets.

00:45:14.630 --> 00:45:17.610
So, not only did I have
a low participation rate,

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:19.480
I actually had a higher withdrawal rate

00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:21.320
where people wanted to help out,

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:24.450
but understandably had to
focus on their businesses

00:45:24.450 --> 00:45:26.313
and were just so busy.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.500
I still try to reach out

00:45:28.500 --> 00:45:31.100
through various Chinese
Associations in the UK,

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:34.440
which some of you might
know as Tongxiang Hui.

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:36.590
I ended up speaking to a very small number

00:45:36.590 --> 00:45:38.420
of participants at the end,

00:45:38.420 --> 00:45:40.700
but not only did they have
a wealth of information

00:45:40.700 --> 00:45:41.740
about themselves,

00:45:41.740 --> 00:45:44.750
it turns out that they had
actually talked among themselves

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:48.680
and several people had information

00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:53.280
that they felt would be really
important for me to know.

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.950
And I really, really
felt so grateful for that

00:45:55.950 --> 00:46:00.050
because they absolutely did not
have to, they really didn't.

00:46:00.050 --> 00:46:03.350
And while this did not
become a core objective

00:46:03.350 --> 00:46:06.050
in my final topic, it
was actually so crucial

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:07.850
in the discussion of the project findings,

00:46:07.850 --> 00:46:09.560
and it was actually sort of
mentioned in the feedback

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:10.913
of my project as well.

00:46:12.150 --> 00:46:14.160
So for my final topic,

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:17.450
I, as you know, evaluated
the museum representations

00:46:17.450 --> 00:46:19.323
of the Chinese diasporas in Britain.

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:23.120
So firstly, I looked at the expectations,

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:24.833
what the curators wanted
out of the project

00:46:24.833 --> 00:46:26.540
through the interviews.

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:28.940
Then I looked at what happened in reality,

00:46:28.940 --> 00:46:32.810
where I looked at my one field visit,

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:35.910
the different transcripts,
the different images,

00:46:35.910 --> 00:46:37.750
and the different perspectives
of what the gallery

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:39.120
actually felt like.

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:41.300
And I was really lucky
that with the images,

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:45.280
it turns out they were
taken between 2013 to 2020.

00:46:45.280 --> 00:46:47.510
So, I had an additional
layer where I could talk

00:46:47.510 --> 00:46:50.070
about how a gallery ages

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:54.010
through seven years.

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:56.640
And finally, I had a
comparative discussion

00:46:56.640 --> 00:46:59.290
of how the Chinese diasporas in Britain

00:46:59.290 --> 00:47:00.530
were actually displayed.

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:03.230
And this was supported by
members of the Chinese community

00:47:03.230 --> 00:47:05.370
in Britain and their
views on how they felt

00:47:05.370 --> 00:47:07.993
when seeing displays
literally about themselves.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:15.663
Changing topics is a really
difficult and personal decision,

00:47:16.500 --> 00:47:19.390
especially when it's a topic
that's deeply connected

00:47:19.390 --> 00:47:20.460
to your background,

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:22.650
and we always feel attached to our topics.

00:47:22.650 --> 00:47:25.550
So, no matter how out of control
the circumstances might be,

00:47:25.550 --> 00:47:26.750
there's always negative feelings

00:47:26.750 --> 00:47:28.350
when something doesn't work out.

00:47:29.490 --> 00:47:32.860
And another thing that I sort
of misunderstood in my process

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:34.980
of pivoting was that it was clean cut,

00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:36.160
that if A doesn't work out,

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:38.050
you're just gonna jump straight to B,

00:47:38.050 --> 00:47:40.350
but it wasn't like that
for me, it was messy,

00:47:40.350 --> 00:47:41.980
it was erratic, gradual,

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:44.033
and it was so emotionally challenging.

00:47:45.100 --> 00:47:47.680
So just to recap the resources I used,

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:50.350
ETHoS is a database of doctoral projects,

00:47:50.350 --> 00:47:52.210
which sometimes even includes the data

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:55.080
that the researchers worked
with, which is brilliant.

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:57.010
I engaged some more unique sources

00:47:57.010 --> 00:48:00.700
like there's a directory of
Chinese Associations in the UK

00:48:00.700 --> 00:48:03.550
where you could basically see which person

00:48:03.550 --> 00:48:07.823
you could contact for
press-related research matters.

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:11.430
There's not expectation that institutions

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:13.050
or individuals should help you,

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:15.520
but I've learned that it
doesn't hurt to contact them.

00:48:15.520 --> 00:48:18.480
And I've been fortunate that
they have helped me a lot,

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:20.370
but I think it needs to
come with an understanding

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:22.080
of your target group.

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:23.070
And like I said,

00:48:23.070 --> 00:48:26.070
for the clarity of my presentation
I've admitted the names,

00:48:26.070 --> 00:48:28.100
but it really took a village

00:48:28.100 --> 00:48:30.580
for this project to come together,

00:48:30.580 --> 00:48:32.970
and I have all these people to thank,

00:48:32.970 --> 00:48:35.180
down to even my sibling

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:38.390
who eventually visited the gallery for me.

00:48:38.390 --> 00:48:40.510
So, I hope that my experience,

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:44.210
I've have been able to offer
some advice and resources,

00:48:44.210 --> 00:48:47.570
but I also hope that
I've been able to offer

00:48:47.570 --> 00:48:49.810
nothing if just emotional solidarity.

00:48:49.810 --> 00:48:50.933
Thank you very much.

00:48:53.640 --> 00:48:55.470
- Great, thank you so much, Tullia.

00:48:55.470 --> 00:48:58.320
So, we are going to
reconvene for the Q&amp;A now.

00:48:58.320 --> 00:48:59.470
If people have questions,

00:48:59.470 --> 00:49:01.570
please feel free to use the Q&amp;A button

00:49:01.570 --> 00:49:02.780
at the bottom of your screen.

00:49:02.780 --> 00:49:04.760
I'd also like to invite
all of our panelists

00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:07.123
to turn their video back on.

00:49:08.010 --> 00:49:09.820
So, I actually just wanted to start off

00:49:09.820 --> 00:49:12.490
with a question of my own

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:13.960
before we get into the questions

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:17.590
that have already been
put in the Q&amp;A function.

00:49:17.590 --> 00:49:22.070
And my question has to do
with what the kind of broader

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:24.440
implications of these research challenges

00:49:24.440 --> 00:49:29.100
are for what the future of
China studies might look like,

00:49:29.100 --> 00:49:32.410
and I'm thinking both
temporarily and geographically.

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:35.240
So, Yi Ci and Bill both mentioned

00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:38.520
that you were originally
thinking of doing a project

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:40.110
more focused on the PRC,

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:43.130
but both of you ended
up moving back in time

00:49:43.130 --> 00:49:46.830
to the late 19th or early 20th century.

00:49:46.830 --> 00:49:50.250
And so my first question is
do you think it's possible

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:53.050
to study the PRC right now?

00:49:53.050 --> 00:49:56.793
And if so, how would
you go about doing that?

00:49:57.930 --> 00:49:59.670
And then my second question has to do

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:02.740
with just kind of the
geographical boundaries

00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:05.750
of thinking about China itself,

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:09.600
Ching Kwan Lee has talked about
the specter of global China,

00:50:09.600 --> 00:50:12.210
so is it do you think perhaps the time

00:50:12.210 --> 00:50:15.660
to start thinking beyond the
bounds of the nation state

00:50:15.660 --> 00:50:18.520
and to think about China in more global,

00:50:18.520 --> 00:50:21.060
or transnational, or
diasphoric dimensions?

00:50:21.060 --> 00:50:23.550
And again, you know, thinking concretely,

00:50:23.550 --> 00:50:25.760
how do we go about doing that?

00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:28.670
So, those are kind of two
but related questions,

00:50:28.670 --> 00:50:30.210
one on the temporal dimension,

00:50:30.210 --> 00:50:32.430
one on the geographical dimension.

00:50:32.430 --> 00:50:36.610
And I just invite all of you to go ahead

00:50:36.610 --> 00:50:37.800
and give your thoughts

00:50:37.800 --> 00:50:41.580
since you are still kind of
in the thick of doing research

00:50:41.580 --> 00:50:43.110
or have recently emerged

00:50:43.110 --> 00:50:45.240
from writing your dissertations or theses.

00:50:45.240 --> 00:50:49.363
So, why don't you go ahead,
and anyone can just jump in.

00:50:54.810 --> 00:50:57.330
- I can take a stab at
the second question.

00:50:57.330 --> 00:51:00.730
I definitely agree that you
don't need to be studying

00:51:00.730 --> 00:51:03.410
geographically what we call China

00:51:03.410 --> 00:51:05.880
to be studying Chinese history.

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:07.130
In my own experience,

00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:10.940
I've found that it was
honestly a lot more informative

00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:13.330
for me to look at what was going on

00:51:13.330 --> 00:51:15.350
in the Chinese communities in the US,

00:51:15.350 --> 00:51:18.690
and see it as a reflection
of what was also going on

00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:20.400
in China during that time,

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:23.130
particularly around different campaigns

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:25.770
for different political
parties, different governments.

00:51:25.770 --> 00:51:28.830
It's not like just when
they came to the US

00:51:28.830 --> 00:51:31.500
those political struggles stopped,

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:35.540
especially with the pretty
dark history of like the US

00:51:35.540 --> 00:51:38.120
treatment towards Chinese immigrants,

00:51:38.120 --> 00:51:40.790
there was a very strong community

00:51:40.790 --> 00:51:44.250
and political organizing spirit

00:51:44.250 --> 00:51:48.190
amongst them that sought
to not only influence

00:51:48.190 --> 00:51:50.530
their US kind of new home,

00:51:50.530 --> 00:51:53.323
but also events back in China.

00:51:58.080 --> 00:52:00.010
- You mentioned me in the first question,

00:52:00.010 --> 00:52:04.300
so I can at least try taking a
stab at the first part of it,

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:07.040
you know, is it possible
to study the PRC today?

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:09.800
I mean, it depends on what
you wanna look at, you know?

00:52:09.800 --> 00:52:13.210
It was not possible for me
to study some of the things

00:52:13.210 --> 00:52:15.680
that I wanted to study
from the perspective

00:52:15.680 --> 00:52:17.590
that I wanted to study them.

00:52:17.590 --> 00:52:20.650
I did not stop my
dissertation in 1979 though,

00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:22.260
because I ran out of material.

00:52:22.260 --> 00:52:23.620
It would have been very easy,

00:52:23.620 --> 00:52:26.130
and in fact, I plan to
continue for my book project

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:28.240
to go further, I kind of stopped it there

00:52:28.240 --> 00:52:31.090
because that's where so much
work had already been done,

00:52:31.090 --> 00:52:34.790
so I kind of wanted to
focus on what I was doing,

00:52:34.790 --> 00:52:39.170
but there was a plethora of materials,

00:52:39.170 --> 00:52:41.390
you know, in Chinese
and Iranian newspapers

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:45.060
talking about minor events
that aren't normally analyzed,

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:45.930
you know, what I call,

00:52:45.930 --> 00:52:48.180
I like to look at what I
call like the combination

00:52:48.180 --> 00:52:51.230
of official and unofficial
relations, so looking at both,

00:52:51.230 --> 00:52:53.670
you know, kind of cultural and
social connections in tandem

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:55.770
with high-level political relations.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:58.580
And that, I mean, there's
plenty more to be done

00:52:58.580 --> 00:53:00.500
just from the same
methods I've been using,

00:53:00.500 --> 00:53:03.043
looking at newspapers, memoirs, travelogs.

00:53:03.043 --> 00:53:06.090
There's a great travelog
that I'm dying to dig into

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:09.670
from a very popular,
prominent Iranian author,

00:53:09.670 --> 00:53:12.320
who went to China in the
1980s and kind of witnessed,

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:15.860
you know, this sort of early changes

00:53:15.860 --> 00:53:18.010
that were going on in that
period and writes about them.

00:53:18.010 --> 00:53:18.843
So I'm really fast,

00:53:18.843 --> 00:53:21.290
you know, there's a
lot to be done I think,

00:53:21.290 --> 00:53:23.660
especially if you're
interested in perspective

00:53:23.660 --> 00:53:26.350
of the outside world on China and the PRC,

00:53:26.350 --> 00:53:28.330
and there's a lot of
unexploited sources out there

00:53:28.330 --> 00:53:29.480
for that kind of stuff.

00:53:32.985 --> 00:53:34.930
- I think I wanna address the question

00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:38.170
on whether it's possible
to study the PRC and how.

00:53:38.170 --> 00:53:42.510
And I think I wanna say
it's very difficult,

00:53:42.510 --> 00:53:44.730
but I wanna say that
there are possibilities

00:53:44.730 --> 00:53:46.990
for doing for studying the PRC.

00:53:46.990 --> 00:53:48.890
I think Emily and I,

00:53:48.890 --> 00:53:51.770
when we talked about me switching topics,

00:53:51.770 --> 00:53:54.873
I did say that we are at
the beginning of a great,

00:53:56.020 --> 00:53:58.820
of a great flood of dissertations
about Republican China

00:53:59.820 --> 00:54:02.390
because it is just easier
to get those sources.

00:54:02.390 --> 00:54:04.690
But I also feel having gone
through this experience

00:54:04.690 --> 00:54:06.350
and looking at what's out there

00:54:06.350 --> 00:54:07.840
and looking at the
possibilities of working

00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:09.070
with what's out there,

00:54:09.070 --> 00:54:11.800
that I'm not going to abandon the PRC,

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:13.000
and I'm not going to abandon

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:15.360
like blood banking in Mao's China.

00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:18.470
I do think there's a possibility
for looking at those,

00:54:18.470 --> 00:54:21.210
looking at the PRC with what is published.

00:54:21.210 --> 00:54:23.480
With blood banking for instance,

00:54:23.480 --> 00:54:26.130
one of the people who are...

00:54:27.380 --> 00:54:29.890
One of the foreigners who
were valorized in the PRC

00:54:29.890 --> 00:54:31.460
was Norman Bethune,

00:54:31.460 --> 00:54:35.550
and the idea of how he had
served China as a doctor.

00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:36.410
And one of the things,

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:39.740
one of the tails that's told
about him is blood donation.

00:54:39.740 --> 00:54:40.860
So, because this is part

00:54:40.860 --> 00:54:42.850
of like the government propaganda, right?

00:54:42.850 --> 00:54:44.850
This is accessible material

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:48.040
that would be kept in a
collection at Hoover for instance,

00:54:48.040 --> 00:54:48.930
that kind of thing.

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:51.260
So, I do think there
are inroads to the PRC,

00:54:51.260 --> 00:54:53.260
you just have to kind of what really hard,

00:54:53.260 --> 00:54:55.950
it's probably harder than
it was five years ago,

00:54:55.950 --> 00:54:59.630
but there are possibilities
out there I think.

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:00.463
Yeah.

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:02.740
- I also wanna mention,

00:55:02.740 --> 00:55:04.270
I'm sure probably a lot
of people know this,

00:55:04.270 --> 00:55:07.350
but there are a lot of Chinese
Foreign Ministry Archive

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:09.900
documents available online,

00:55:09.900 --> 00:55:12.850
some of them are even conveniently
translated into English.

00:55:14.060 --> 00:55:15.510
I don't know if one of
you guys can remember,

00:55:15.510 --> 00:55:16.710
I'm blanking on the website

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:19.490
that I think it's through
the Hoover Institute though,

00:55:19.490 --> 00:55:23.740
but there are documents that
were kind of taken or scanned

00:55:23.740 --> 00:55:26.100
out of Foreign Ministry Archives
before they closed down,

00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:28.940
and they are a lifeline to researchers

00:55:28.940 --> 00:55:30.930
trying to piece together
what's going on right now.

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:33.990
- And the Wilson Center as well.

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:35.167
- That was it, the Wilson Center.

00:55:35.167 --> 00:55:36.000
- Yeah.
- That's what I was thinking

00:55:36.000 --> 00:55:36.833
of, thank you.

00:55:36.833 --> 00:55:39.260
But yeah, they're a great
source for stuff like that

00:55:39.260 --> 00:55:40.660
and is growing all the time.

00:55:42.550 --> 00:55:46.560
- Maybe I can also chime in on
the question of PRC history.

00:55:46.560 --> 00:55:47.640
My name is Denise Ho,

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:49.980
and I teach Modern
Chinese History at Yale,

00:55:49.980 --> 00:55:52.780
I'm one of the co-organizers
of this series.

00:55:52.780 --> 00:55:55.610
I think maybe, or the
way I would think about

00:55:55.610 --> 00:55:57.580
the first part of Emily's question

00:55:57.580 --> 00:55:59.190
is like with a metaphor

00:55:59.190 --> 00:56:01.380
and then with looking at other fields.

00:56:01.380 --> 00:56:04.460
So, the metaphor is just thinking about

00:56:04.460 --> 00:56:06.630
like the number of pixels on a screen.

00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:09.770
And I think when people look back

00:56:09.770 --> 00:56:12.800
and then are nostalgic
at kind of a golden age

00:56:12.800 --> 00:56:14.580
of research in Chinese history,

00:56:14.580 --> 00:56:17.100
maybe that in the 90s and the early 00s,

00:56:17.100 --> 00:56:20.830
I think people are get very excited

00:56:20.830 --> 00:56:22.280
about what they were able to find.

00:56:22.280 --> 00:56:24.470
And that the truth

00:56:24.470 --> 00:56:26.370
is that we're just not in
that situation anymore.

00:56:26.370 --> 00:56:28.930
So, I think that the people on this panel

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:31.430
have pointed to many of the different ways

00:56:31.430 --> 00:56:34.450
in which you can still get
enough pixels to make a picture

00:56:34.450 --> 00:56:35.740
so that you can still see an image

00:56:35.740 --> 00:56:38.430
that describes something that is a real

00:56:38.430 --> 00:56:40.970
and true story of Chinese history.

00:56:40.970 --> 00:56:42.990
It might not have the
same grain of detail,

00:56:42.990 --> 00:56:45.360
or it might require shifting

00:56:45.360 --> 00:56:50.360
someone used the metaphor of
the aperture shifting focus,

00:56:50.500 --> 00:56:51.820
but I think it's still possible,

00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:53.780
and I certainly hope it's still possible.

00:56:53.780 --> 00:56:55.270
And the second thing
that I think is useful

00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:58.870
to think about is the way the
social scientists before us

00:56:58.870 --> 00:57:01.490
did histories of the PRC,

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:04.970
but they were doing
field work with emigres

00:57:04.970 --> 00:57:07.280
maybe in sort of taking out
some of the methodologies

00:57:07.280 --> 00:57:11.240
that Tullia was describing,
or looking at newspapers.

00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:13.010
So, I think this is done,

00:57:13.010 --> 00:57:17.390
that this is not completely
unprecedented for us,

00:57:17.390 --> 00:57:18.860
and so we should take inspiration

00:57:18.860 --> 00:57:20.660
from many of these different fields.

00:57:23.000 --> 00:57:26.960
- Yeah, that's great
insights from all of you.

00:57:26.960 --> 00:57:29.440
And again, I mean, I'd also
just like to offer encouragement

00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:31.630
in the sense that there have
been so many more sources

00:57:31.630 --> 00:57:33.210
that have been digitized recently,

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:35.330
and that are typically accessible

00:57:35.330 --> 00:57:39.010
through university
databases like newspapers

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:40.450
like Renmin Ribao,

00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:43.620
or databases like Dousho,
all of these things,

00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:46.130
I mean, a lot of these
sources are kind of mediated

00:57:46.130 --> 00:57:47.040
in a particular way,

00:57:47.040 --> 00:57:47.977
but all sources are mediated

00:57:47.977 --> 00:57:50.150
and there are ways of
still working with them

00:57:50.150 --> 00:57:53.350
and developing a fruitful topic.

00:57:53.350 --> 00:57:54.860
So, I'll turn to one of the questions

00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:56.480
that was asked in the Q&amp;A,

00:57:56.480 --> 00:57:59.730
this comes from Lu Hwang who asks

00:58:00.640 --> 00:58:05.220
that about the issue of trying to strike

00:58:05.220 --> 00:58:09.920
a good balance on taking a
wide view versus a deep dive

00:58:09.920 --> 00:58:12.830
into a specific topic,

00:58:12.830 --> 00:58:16.020
Lu working on Indian and
Tibetan Buddhist philosophy,

00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:18.450
as well as Chinese Buddhist history,

00:58:18.450 --> 00:58:20.740
they're trying to figure out a topic

00:58:20.740 --> 00:58:23.090
that can both address their
broad research interests,

00:58:23.090 --> 00:58:25.870
but not be too broad to be superficial.

00:58:25.870 --> 00:58:29.100
And so, do you have any
insights about how to strike

00:58:29.100 --> 00:58:33.190
a good balance between
adopting kind of a broad view,

00:58:33.190 --> 00:58:36.260
but also being able to
get into the nitty gritty

00:58:36.260 --> 00:58:39.900
of the detail of Chinese
history or Chinese philosophy

00:58:39.900 --> 00:58:42.373
that we're all really passionate about?

00:58:45.463 --> 00:58:46.848
- I can go ahead and start

00:58:46.848 --> 00:58:49.660
just 'cause I talked a lot
about broad topics, right?

00:58:49.660 --> 00:58:53.020
I think that like it's obviously
you don't wanna go too far

00:58:53.020 --> 00:58:53.860
in the opposite direction,

00:58:53.860 --> 00:58:56.350
you don't want something that's too broad,

00:58:56.350 --> 00:59:00.060
you don't wanna try to
answer an entire field.

00:59:00.060 --> 00:59:01.780
You know, one thing I was blessed with

00:59:01.780 --> 00:59:03.330
was the fact that my field is wide open,

00:59:03.330 --> 00:59:05.080
so I kind of decide what it was,

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:07.180
you know, not many people have really done

00:59:07.180 --> 00:59:10.080
Sino-Iranian history
in this period before.

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:12.170
But, you know, the approach that I took

00:59:12.170 --> 00:59:15.510
was really it's not like
I didn't do deep dives,

00:59:15.510 --> 00:59:18.070
it's really more that I kind
of broadened my perspective

00:59:18.070 --> 00:59:21.653
and did several deep dives
on different periods history

00:59:21.653 --> 00:59:24.810
and kind of put that
together into a narrative.

00:59:24.810 --> 00:59:27.570
You know, so like my early
chapter is on a very brief,

00:59:27.570 --> 00:59:31.919
you know, 1905-1911 moment,
where Iranian and Chinese

00:59:31.919 --> 00:59:33.930
constitutionalists are
talking to one another.

00:59:33.930 --> 00:59:37.737
My second chapter is on the
1920s to the early 1940s,

00:59:37.737 --> 00:59:42.170
you know, my third chapter
takes a chunk of 1949 to 1959.

00:59:42.170 --> 00:59:43.210
And then the fourth chapter,

00:59:43.210 --> 00:59:45.400
I do it that deep dive into Iranian Maoism

00:59:45.400 --> 00:59:46.830
that I wanted to do.

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:50.490
You know, so I would say that
when you're thinking broadly,

00:59:50.490 --> 00:59:54.050
you just kind of wanna think
about what you can do more

00:59:54.050 --> 00:59:55.970
rather than like how can I?

00:59:55.970 --> 00:59:57.900
You know, you don't wanna be
up in the stratosphere, right?

00:59:57.900 --> 01:00:01.433
You wanna have something in
each chapter grounding you

01:00:01.433 --> 01:00:03.290
to something more specific.

01:00:03.290 --> 01:00:06.040
That's the best advice that
I think I could give on that.

01:00:07.320 --> 01:00:10.683
- Does anyone else wanna
weigh in on that question?

01:00:13.273 --> 01:00:15.310
- I'll just say I also think your advisor

01:00:15.310 --> 01:00:18.130
plays a really important role in this.

01:00:18.130 --> 01:00:20.530
I was very fortunate that my advisor and I

01:00:20.530 --> 01:00:23.560
had a great communication system set up,

01:00:23.560 --> 01:00:26.530
constantly on WeChat
chatting with each other.

01:00:26.530 --> 01:00:29.180
And at times he was just
kind of brutally honest

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:31.280
if I was straying from my question

01:00:31.280 --> 01:00:32.980
and really trying to keep me pinned

01:00:32.980 --> 01:00:37.560
on what I was actually trying to research,

01:00:37.560 --> 01:00:40.430
and not kind of squirreling
off in different directions,

01:00:40.430 --> 01:00:42.630
which are personally interesting to me,

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:43.960
but at the end of the day,

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:47.070
I needed to write a cohesive document

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:49.263
that that would pass defense.

01:00:53.330 --> 01:00:56.660
- So, I see that we're
actually getting some links

01:00:56.660 --> 01:01:01.380
in the chat about other types
of sources or databases.

01:01:01.380 --> 01:01:04.930
We kept the chat open specifically
so that people can share

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:07.350
their advice and their
resources with others,

01:01:07.350 --> 01:01:08.770
but just when you do so,

01:01:08.770 --> 01:01:11.840
remember to hit the button that says

01:01:11.840 --> 01:01:14.370
send chat to panelists and attendees

01:01:14.370 --> 01:01:16.330
because otherwise it will
just come to the panelists

01:01:16.330 --> 01:01:17.660
and the attendees won't see it.

01:01:17.660 --> 01:01:20.800
But if you have resources, or databases

01:01:20.800 --> 01:01:22.370
or, you know, libraries,

01:01:22.370 --> 01:01:24.610
or any type of online material

01:01:24.610 --> 01:01:26.650
that you think might be
interesting for others to see,

01:01:26.650 --> 01:01:28.210
please feel free to drop it in the chat,

01:01:28.210 --> 01:01:29.900
we can also try to compile those

01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:31.960
and send those out in a follow-up email

01:01:31.960 --> 01:01:34.173
to all of the registered participants.

01:01:35.950 --> 01:01:40.280
So, we got another
question from a librarian

01:01:40.280 --> 01:01:44.240
asking about from your perspective

01:01:44.240 --> 01:01:47.100
how librarians can make the best use

01:01:47.100 --> 01:01:50.720
of their limited resources
and limited staffing,

01:01:50.720 --> 01:01:54.500
so what would you particularly like to see

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:56.640
librarians do during this time

01:01:56.640 --> 01:01:58.830
to try to serve as many
people as possible,

01:01:58.830 --> 01:02:00.660
even with the types of funding

01:02:00.660 --> 01:02:03.210
and staffing constraints
that they might be facing?

01:02:04.630 --> 01:02:05.940
What would have been helpful for you

01:02:05.940 --> 01:02:07.893
in the process of your research?

01:02:12.090 --> 01:02:13.873
- I could take a stab at that.

01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:18.870
I am coming from this as
someone who's in museums,

01:02:18.870 --> 01:02:21.670
so I can sort of relate quite intimately

01:02:21.670 --> 01:02:25.790
with the issues of funding,
or rather the lack thereof.

01:02:25.790 --> 01:02:28.950
And I do agree that there
is a continuing demand

01:02:28.950 --> 01:02:31.310
for more digitization to be done.

01:02:31.310 --> 01:02:35.310
And in my research project,
I would have loved to have,

01:02:35.310 --> 01:02:38.303
for example, shipping logs
or immigration records,

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:41.310
that would have been
really, really helpful,

01:02:41.310 --> 01:02:42.400
but at the same time,

01:02:42.400 --> 01:02:45.853
having worked in museums
during a pandemic actually,

01:02:46.690 --> 01:02:49.390
I understand also it's
really, really difficult.

01:02:49.390 --> 01:02:52.646
And that funding often
relies on external sources,

01:02:52.646 --> 01:02:54.290
like especially in the UK,

01:02:54.290 --> 01:02:56.340
which is a system I'm more familiar with.

01:02:58.010 --> 01:02:59.900
So, there are a number of documents

01:02:59.900 --> 01:03:01.170
that I would have loved to have had,

01:03:01.170 --> 01:03:05.960
or maybe even just maybe
like a sort of directory

01:03:05.960 --> 01:03:07.990
of the sources that
would have been available

01:03:07.990 --> 01:03:10.850
as opposed to actual
scans of the resources.

01:03:10.850 --> 01:03:13.040
That being said, I think
there is an element

01:03:13.040 --> 01:03:15.620
of understanding that needs to be had

01:03:15.620 --> 01:03:17.610
between researchers as well as people

01:03:17.610 --> 01:03:19.820
who provide access to research,

01:03:19.820 --> 01:03:21.960
that it is a difficult time for all of us,

01:03:21.960 --> 01:03:25.300
that especially with libraries,
museums, and archives,

01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:26.560
funding's getting smaller.

01:03:26.560 --> 01:03:29.140
There's more dispute politically

01:03:29.140 --> 01:03:31.580
about how to for example,

01:03:31.580 --> 01:03:35.260
just make, for example,
colonial documents available.

01:03:35.260 --> 01:03:38.260
So, I think that there is
an element of understanding

01:03:38.260 --> 01:03:41.410
to be had here that we
need to be kind pretty much

01:03:41.410 --> 01:03:43.720
and be okay that some documents

01:03:43.720 --> 01:03:46.763
just won't be available to
us digitally for awhile.

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:54.990
- I can also just jump
in and say that recently

01:03:54.990 --> 01:03:56.620
I've been fairly frustrated

01:03:56.620 --> 01:04:00.180
about not being able to go
to archives or libraries,

01:04:00.180 --> 01:04:03.450
even just in the region
of Southern California.

01:04:03.450 --> 01:04:06.910
And what I've started doing
is reaching out personally

01:04:06.910 --> 01:04:10.690
to archivists telling them
what my research interests are,

01:04:10.690 --> 01:04:14.900
identifying particular databases

01:04:14.900 --> 01:04:17.180
or sources that I'm interested in seeing.

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:19.470
And like 9 times out of 10,

01:04:19.470 --> 01:04:21.120
I will get a response from the archivist,

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:22.770
and they've been really wonderful

01:04:22.770 --> 01:04:26.950
about scanning these materials
and emailing them to me,

01:04:26.950 --> 01:04:28.400
which is fantastic.

01:04:28.400 --> 01:04:32.450
It doesn't hurt to ask, you
might not get a response,

01:04:32.450 --> 01:04:34.920
you might not be able to
acquire all of the materials

01:04:34.920 --> 01:04:36.220
that you're interested in,

01:04:36.220 --> 01:04:39.080
but it is certainly
going to be more fruitful

01:04:39.080 --> 01:04:41.030
than if you'd just don't ask at all,

01:04:41.030 --> 01:04:43.033
so why not send an email?

01:04:43.950 --> 01:04:45.310
Denise did you wanna jump in as well?

01:04:45.310 --> 01:04:46.510
- I just wanted to throw out

01:04:46.510 --> 01:04:48.493
a couple of other resources out there.

01:04:49.570 --> 01:04:52.560
The Librarian for Digital Projects

01:04:52.560 --> 01:04:54.570
at the Fairbank Center
at Harvard University,

01:04:54.570 --> 01:04:58.410
who's been a supporter of this program.

01:04:58.410 --> 01:05:01.210
Kwok-Leong Tang is organizing,

01:05:01.210 --> 01:05:03.170
has been organizing a series of Q&amp;A

01:05:03.170 --> 01:05:05.110
like open office hours

01:05:05.110 --> 01:05:07.390
where a group of librarians
actually get together

01:05:07.390 --> 01:05:08.860
to answer questions.

01:05:08.860 --> 01:05:11.380
So, I think that is a
really great resource.

01:05:11.380 --> 01:05:13.160
I know that some history departments,

01:05:13.160 --> 01:05:15.880
the Nanjing Technological
University History Department

01:05:15.880 --> 01:05:20.360
has like crowdsourced a
spreadsheet of digital sources,

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:23.160
so I think there's a
lot of stuff out there

01:05:23.160 --> 01:05:27.040
and just as many platforms

01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:30.400
or not sharing knowledge
about what platforms

01:05:30.400 --> 01:05:32.690
are out there for extra

01:05:35.190 --> 01:05:38.160
lists of digitized sources
would be very helpful.

01:05:38.160 --> 01:05:41.710
I think in some cases
there's a concern about

01:05:41.710 --> 01:05:44.580
well, we can't publicly
share archives for example

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:47.230
because of the rules
that we have to adhere

01:05:47.230 --> 01:05:49.020
to when we were originally

01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:51.150
looking at those are archival materials.

01:05:51.150 --> 01:05:52.820
But for published
sources, I think certainly

01:05:52.820 --> 01:05:55.093
there are a lot of resources out there.

01:05:57.730 --> 01:06:01.080
I wonder if I can ask the
panelists a question about

01:06:02.580 --> 01:06:03.920
how you're...

01:06:04.770 --> 01:06:07.220
Maybe from the point
of view of the advisors

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:09.200
and the departments, in what ways

01:06:12.050 --> 01:06:15.150
where are your advisors
or departments helpful

01:06:15.150 --> 01:06:17.460
in the process of the research pivot?

01:06:17.460 --> 01:06:21.423
And in what ways could they
have offered more support?

01:06:23.520 --> 01:06:26.930
- I could go first, mainly
because I had, as I said,

01:06:26.930 --> 01:06:31.230
quite a messy sort of transition
process between my topics.

01:06:31.230 --> 01:06:33.080
The thing I was most grateful
for was just the amount

01:06:33.080 --> 01:06:35.973
of patience that my advisors had for me.

01:06:37.170 --> 01:06:39.530
And a lot of advisors
are brilliant in that way

01:06:39.530 --> 01:06:40.890
in just offering emotional support

01:06:40.890 --> 01:06:43.080
and basically letting you
just sound ideas off them

01:06:43.080 --> 01:06:44.650
again and again, and again,

01:06:44.650 --> 01:06:46.013
until something works out.

01:06:47.670 --> 01:06:50.300
And I think I struggled sort of asking

01:06:50.300 --> 01:06:53.370
for more from my advisors

01:06:53.370 --> 01:06:56.503
when considering that I
did my topic in a pandemic,

01:06:57.930 --> 01:06:59.923
that being said, I think,

01:07:00.820 --> 01:07:04.590
you know, it would have been
nice to be able to connect

01:07:04.590 --> 01:07:08.360
with maybe even more subject specialists

01:07:08.360 --> 01:07:10.180
or if I could be referred to other people

01:07:10.180 --> 01:07:13.413
who might know be more well-versed
in the topic that I knew,

01:07:14.320 --> 01:07:16.100
but it feels like I'm complaining

01:07:16.100 --> 01:07:18.460
in what was a really bad situation.

01:07:18.460 --> 01:07:21.830
So yeah, in the course
of doing a dissertation

01:07:21.830 --> 01:07:23.790
in the pandemic the
thing I was most grateful

01:07:23.790 --> 01:07:26.830
for was actually just the
support and the patience.

01:07:26.830 --> 01:07:29.630
And I ended up finding, as I mentioned,

01:07:29.630 --> 01:07:32.350
a lot of people externally who helped me.

01:07:32.350 --> 01:07:34.900
So, the only sort of gripe that I had,

01:07:34.900 --> 01:07:36.100
I've been able to solve

01:07:36.100 --> 01:07:38.223
by approaching individuals myself as well.

01:07:44.010 --> 01:07:47.160
- Yeah, I mean, I also
am very, very thankful

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:49.740
for my advisor, he was fantastic,

01:07:49.740 --> 01:07:52.810
but I think something which is
more on the departmental side

01:07:52.810 --> 01:07:57.700
is just flexibility in terms
of some of the more nitpicky

01:07:57.700 --> 01:08:01.030
regulations that govern
whether it's defense

01:08:01.030 --> 01:08:04.560
or how to kind of properly present things.

01:08:04.560 --> 01:08:05.960
Speaking from personal experience,

01:08:05.960 --> 01:08:08.870
a lot of those hadn't been updated

01:08:08.870 --> 01:08:12.040
a year and a half into the pandemic.

01:08:12.040 --> 01:08:14.510
So, when it came time for defense,

01:08:14.510 --> 01:08:16.270
there was a lot of scrambling to be done

01:08:16.270 --> 01:08:19.450
because there wasn't consideration

01:08:19.450 --> 01:08:20.780
that we would be doing it online,

01:08:20.780 --> 01:08:23.000
or digitally, or all of that.

01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:25.840
And so I think just I wish I had known

01:08:25.840 --> 01:08:29.000
kind of at the beginning
what that might entail,

01:08:29.000 --> 01:08:30.400
but also at the departmental level,

01:08:30.400 --> 01:08:32.890
just really thinking
through the different stages

01:08:32.890 --> 01:08:35.160
that your students are going through,

01:08:35.160 --> 01:08:38.840
and trying to build the
infrastructure for them to succeed.

01:08:43.080 --> 01:08:45.070
- Okay, I should disclose

01:08:45.070 --> 01:08:48.110
that one of my advisors is on this,

01:08:48.110 --> 01:08:50.760
is one of the co-organizers
of this webinar (laughs).

01:08:52.270 --> 01:08:53.870
So, I mean, I definitely I mean,

01:08:53.870 --> 01:08:57.130
I agree that, you know,
talking with your advisors

01:08:57.130 --> 01:08:59.640
is one of the things you have to do

01:08:59.640 --> 01:09:02.440
when you decide you're going
to severely veer off the topic

01:09:02.440 --> 01:09:05.170
that you proposed to do
for your dissertation.

01:09:05.170 --> 01:09:08.113
And I, you know,

01:09:09.570 --> 01:09:12.470
my advisors were very
supportive when I told them

01:09:12.470 --> 01:09:14.880
about the difficulties I was facing.

01:09:14.880 --> 01:09:18.470
I said, "Look, I tried and then
this is what happened to me,

01:09:18.470 --> 01:09:20.020
but these are the new sources I found,

01:09:20.020 --> 01:09:23.180
and this is something that
I think I can work with."

01:09:23.180 --> 01:09:26.590
And it was a difficult conversation

01:09:26.590 --> 01:09:28.920
because I had to deal with
like kind of the disappointment

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:31.240
of not doing the original topic,

01:09:31.240 --> 01:09:33.483
but also talking to them
about something completely new

01:09:33.483 --> 01:09:35.723
that I had not done before that point.

01:09:37.280 --> 01:09:38.810
But, you know, they were very supportive

01:09:38.810 --> 01:09:41.280
and I think I got great
feedback on the topic,

01:09:41.280 --> 01:09:44.060
and especially when I was
writing the early chapters

01:09:44.060 --> 01:09:46.208
to get comments on how it was shaping up

01:09:46.208 --> 01:09:50.000
was really, really crucial for me

01:09:50.000 --> 01:09:52.150
because for me, I was like being,

01:09:52.150 --> 01:09:53.830
I was suddenly embarking on a new topic,

01:09:53.830 --> 01:09:56.300
it wasn't a topic that I
had been thinking about

01:09:56.300 --> 01:09:58.760
for like years, so that
was really, really scary.

01:09:58.760 --> 01:10:00.780
And to have that kind of support

01:10:00.780 --> 01:10:03.140
and that kind of feedback
at that formative stage

01:10:03.140 --> 01:10:04.970
for my project was essential,

01:10:04.970 --> 01:10:07.600
I think I wouldn't be
so close to the end now

01:10:07.600 --> 01:10:10.000
if I didn't have that support.

01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:12.790
And in terms of my department,

01:10:12.790 --> 01:10:15.570
I think actually I've really been thankful

01:10:15.570 --> 01:10:17.700
for how the department
has been supporting me

01:10:17.700 --> 01:10:19.560
in terms of understanding
that grad students

01:10:19.560 --> 01:10:21.700
would be like just
scattered around the world

01:10:21.700 --> 01:10:23.373
and having to do everything digitally.

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:28.340
So, I think that I've been
very lucky with my department

01:10:28.340 --> 01:10:31.630
that I've been supported kind
of administratively that way

01:10:31.630 --> 01:10:33.450
as somebody who couldn't go back to the US

01:10:33.450 --> 01:10:36.770
and then I'm just completing
from overseas now.

01:10:36.770 --> 01:10:37.853
So, yeah.

01:10:42.926 --> 01:10:43.960
- Great.

01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:47.160
Well, I think we are
just about out of time,

01:10:47.160 --> 01:10:48.470
right at 75 minutes,

01:10:48.470 --> 01:10:51.180
so I would like to once again

01:10:51.180 --> 01:10:53.180
thank our wonderful panelists

01:10:53.180 --> 01:10:55.950
for providing their
experiences and insights.

01:10:55.950 --> 01:10:58.560
I'd like to thank the Council
on East Asian Studies at Yale,

01:10:58.560 --> 01:11:00.870
especially Stephanie Kim and Richard Sosa,

01:11:00.870 --> 01:11:03.420
who provided all the staffing
and support for this event.

01:11:03.420 --> 01:11:04.440
And of course, I would like to thank

01:11:04.440 --> 01:11:06.420
all of you for attending.

01:11:06.420 --> 01:11:10.080
Our next webinar in this
series will be on Monday,

01:11:10.080 --> 01:11:13.780
July 19th at 7:30 PM Eastern.

01:11:13.780 --> 01:11:14.990
It will be on the topic

01:11:14.990 --> 01:11:19.290
of Archives, Libraries
and Databases in Taiwan.

01:11:19.290 --> 01:11:22.000
Please note that you
will have to re-register

01:11:22.000 --> 01:11:24.990
for that webinar in order
to receive the link,

01:11:24.990 --> 01:11:26.320
it is not the same link

01:11:26.320 --> 01:11:29.110
as the one that we used for this webinar.

01:11:29.110 --> 01:11:32.180
You can go to the Yale Council
on East Asian Studies page

01:11:32.180 --> 01:11:34.530
that Denise has just dropped in the chat,

01:11:34.530 --> 01:11:36.400
and you can re-register for that.

01:11:36.400 --> 01:11:38.490
Also, just as a heads up the next webinar

01:11:38.490 --> 01:11:40.970
will at least partly be in Chinese

01:11:40.970 --> 01:11:43.080
because we'll be hosting
several archivists

01:11:43.080 --> 01:11:45.060
and librarians from Taiwan,

01:11:45.060 --> 01:11:47.080
so just as a word of warning

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:50.100
you will need to speak
a little bit of Chinese

01:11:50.100 --> 01:11:51.830
in order to understand.

01:11:51.830 --> 01:11:53.690
But thank you again for tuning in,

01:11:53.690 --> 01:11:55.900
and hopefully we will see you all again

01:11:55.900 --> 01:11:57.690
in a couple of weeks.

01:11:57.690 --> 01:11:58.523
So, take care.

